<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1894 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Torna a Surriento</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Neapolitan song from the 1890s that asks a loved one to come back to the seaside town of Sorrento.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Giambattista De Curtis</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ernesto De Curtis</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Sorrento, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1894 (by tradition 1902)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Giambattista De Curtis, deposited 1894, copyrighted officially 1905)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torna-a-surriento">Torna a Surriento</a>&quot; (&quot;Come back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a>&quot;) is a Neapolitan song composed by the Italian musician Ernesto De Curtis to words by his brother, the poet and painter Giambattista De Curtis. Tradition holds that it was written in 1902, when Guglielmo Tramontano, the mayor of Sorrento, asked his friend Giambattista to write a song for the Prime Minister, Giuseppe Zanardelli, who was then staying at Tramontano's seaside hotel, the Imperial Hotel Tramontano; it was said to celebrate Zanardelli's stay, and some say it was a plea to him to keep his promise to help the poor town, which needed above all a sewage system. More recent research suggests the song was only reworked for the occasion: family papers show that the brothers had deposited a copy with the Italian Society of Authors and Editors in 1894, eight years before they said they wrote it. It was officially copyrighted in 1905.</p><p>It became one of the most popular songs of the Neapolitan tradition, beside &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio</a>&quot;, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a>&quot; and &quot;Santa Lucia&quot;. Claude Aveling wrote English words for it, &quot;Come Back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a>&quot;, and it has been sung by performers of many kinds: the musician Bono ends his film Bono: Stories of Surrender (2025) by singing its last two verses, after remembering his father, a tenor. It was heard throughout the 1954 film Seagulls Over Sorrento, played on the concertina.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A love song set against the beauty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> and its surroundings, asking a loved one to come back. The mood is longing and tender.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Neapolitan</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Neapolitan</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Vide 'o mare quant’è bello,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See the sea, how beautiful it is,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">spira tanto sentimento,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it breathes so much feeling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Comme tu a chi tiene mente,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as you do to whoever looks at you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ca scetato 'o faje sunnà.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>making them dream while awake.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Guarda, gua’ chistu ciardino;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look, look at this garden;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Siente, sie’ sti sciure arance:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Smell, smell these orange blossoms:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Nu profumo accussì fino</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A scent so fine</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Dinto 'o core se ne va…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>goes into the heart…</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">E tu dice: &quot;I’ parto, addio!&quot;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And you say: &quot;I am leaving, goodbye!&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">T’alluntane da 'stu core…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You go away from this heart…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Da la terra de l’ammore…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the land of love…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Tiene 'o core 'e nun turnà?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have you the heart not to come back?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ma nun me lassà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But do not leave me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Nun darme stu turmiento!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not give me this torment!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Torna a Surriento,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come back to Sorrento,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">famme campà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let me live!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Vide 'o mare de Surriento,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See the sea of Sorrento,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">che tesore tene 'nfunno:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what treasures it holds at its bottom:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">chi ha girato tutto 'o munno</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whoever has travelled the whole world</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">nun l'ha visto comm'a ccà.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>has not seen it as it is here.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Guarda attuorno sti ssirene,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look around at these sirens,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">ca te guardano 'ncantate,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who look at you enchanted,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">e te vonno tantu bene...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and love you so much...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Te vulessero vasà.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They would like to kiss you.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">E tu dice: &quot;I' parto, addio!&quot;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And you say: &quot;I am leaving, goodbye!&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">T'alluntane da 'stu core</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You go away from this heart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Da la terra de l'ammore</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the land of love</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Tiene 'o core 'e nun turnà?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Have you the heart not to come back?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Ma nun me lassà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But do not leave me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Nun darme stu turmiento!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not give me this torment!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Torna a Surriento,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come back to Sorrento,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Famme campà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let me live!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Giambattista De Curtis, the Neapolitan text as quoted on Italian Wikipedia. Text from <a href="https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torna_a_Surriento" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>Surriento is the Neapolitan name of Sorrento, on the Bay of Naples.</li><li>Lines 1.3-1.4: literally 'like you to whoever keeps you in mind (looks at you), / whom, awake, you make dream'.</li><li>Line 2.2: siente means both 'hear' and 'smell'; sciure arance, orange flowers.</li><li>Lines 3.4 and 7.4: tiene 'o core 'e nun turnà?, literally 'do you have the heart not to return?'.</li><li>Line 4.4: famme campà, literally 'make me live', 'let me live'.</li><li>Line 5.4: comm'a ccà, 'as here', i.e. the like of what is here.</li><li>Line 6.1: sirene, the sirens of myth.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
